      "text": "To solve for the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we start by determining the value of \\(n\\), the median of the set. Since there are five numbers in the set, the median is the third number, which is \\(n + 6\\).\n\nGiven that the median is 9, we can set up the equation:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 9\n\\]\nSolving for \\(n\\), we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\nn = 3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(n = 3\\), we can substitute this value into the set to find the actual numbers:\n\\[\n\\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the mean of this set. The mean is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements. First, we find the sum of the elements:\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\n\\]\nThere are 5 elements in the set, so the mean is:\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the mean of the set is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
